<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teuta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 231–228 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teuta was queen of the Ardiaean kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, ruling as regent for her stepson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinnes">Pinnes</a> after the death of her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a> in 231 BCE. Illyrian raids on Greek cities and Italian traders under her rule, and the killing of a Roman envoy, provoked the First Illyrian War in 229 BCE. Defeated, she accepted Roman terms in 228 BCE that limited Illyrian shipping south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lissus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teuta.jpg" alt="Teuta" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Teuta_(Antonio_Salamanca).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Her name is attested in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Teuta, a form of the Illyrian *Teuta(na), &quot;queen&quot;, literally &quot;mistress of the people&quot;. After the death of her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardiaei">Ardiaei</a>, in 231 BCE she acted as regent for her stepson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinnes">Pinnes</a>. The kingdom stretched along the Adriatic coast from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a> river and probably controlled most of the Illyrian interior. The ancient sources, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> who were generally hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>, bias what is known of her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, Teuta told her commanders to treat all neighbouring states as enemies and supported the piratical raids of her subjects. In 231 BCE her armies attacked Elis and Messenia and then captured the Epirote city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenice">Phoenice</a>, which was soon freed against a fee and freeborn prisoners. Illyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> looted so many Italian merchant ships that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> sent ambassadors to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scodra</a>. Polybius says Teuta told them piracy was a traditional custom she could not end, and that one envoy answered insolently and was murdered on the voyage home, allegedly on her order. In Appian's account two envoys were killed by Illyrian lemboi while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a> was still alive, so the interview may not have taken place.</p><p>In 229 BCE Rome declared war and for the first time sent armies across the Adriatic, about 20,000 troops and 200 ships under Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus and Lucius Postumius Albinus. Her governor Demetrius surrendered Corcyra to them, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scodra</a> while Teuta withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/risan">Rhizon</a>. Polybius reports that in early spring 228 BCE she agreed to pay an annual tribute, to rule only north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lissus</a>, now Lezhë, and not to sail beyond it with more than two unarmed ships. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> she abdicated in 227 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Appian says that after the defeat Teuta sent an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to return captives and to apologise for what happened under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a>, not under her. Marjeta Šašel Kos considers Appian's the most objective portrait, and Peter Derow finds his story of the murder of the envoys more plausible than that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, whose narrative was written almost a century later and probably rests on Fabius Pictor. A legend rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/risan">Risan</a> holds that she ended her life in grief by throwing herself from the Orjen mountains at Lipci. Teuta is a common given name among Albanian women, and the club Teuta Durrës was named after her in 1930.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teuta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Illyrian raiders under Queen <b>Teuta</b> sacked Phoenice in 230 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/epirote-league">Epirote League</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Teuta's</b> attacks on Italian traders led to the First Illyrian War in 229 BCE, and Demetrius of Pharos provoked a second in 219 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-kingdom">Illyrian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>By the later 3rd century BCE their lands lay within the Illyrian Kingdom of Agron and <b>Teuta</b>, and after 229 BCE much of the coast came under Roman protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/taulantii">Taulantii</a></li></ul></section>
